ted States (Iridium 33, active telecom satellite); companies: Iridium Communications (U.S.) and Russian state operators; insurers, FCC? (regulators). \n**Major influences on the decision:** Outer Space Treaty 1967; Convention on International Liability 1972; national space law; debris-tracking data; safety of ISS; property damage; responsibility for defunct objects.\n**Potential outcomes if space law did not exist:** No liability rule; no compensation; no shared data; no debris-mitigation duty; more accidents; blame; higher costs; unsafe operations.\n\nNeed maybe \"decision\" in question: \"major influences on the decision\" — decision could be which party is responsible? In Iridium, no formal legal decision. We can say \"decision: liability assessment/claim handling\". But no actual claim? Iridium did not file claim? We can say \"decision/claim framework\". Use \"decision\" maybe: \"the decision to treat the collision as a liability case\". Or if we choose a more legal case with actual decision? Let's search memory: \"1997 US House Committee? no.\" \"1999 Russian Space Research?\" Hmm.\n\nCould use \"1978? The Soviet Union damaged a US satellite? no.\" There is \"1963? US-Soviet satellite debris?\" The Liability Convention has not been used much. There is \"2013? European Commission v. France?\" Not space law. \"Space law case\" likely not necessarily court case; they ask research a space law case. Iridium/Cosmos is common.\n\nMaybe use \"1978: Canada's OSATR?\" No.\n\nMaybe \"1996: The case of the failed Russian Antasat?\" Hmm.\n\nCould research \"Iridium 33-Cosmos 2251 collision\" and call it case. Need answer \"major influences on the decision\" maybe if there was a claim? Iridium likely didn't file formal liability claim; but legal analysis decided Russia responsible under Liability Convention because state responsibility? Let's be careful. If Iridium did not file claim, maybe no decision. Could say \"decision under review: who bears liability?\" But user likel